From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: move rx_channel with a channel switch

ar->rx_channel is set when a channel context is added and cleared when it
is removed, but a channel switch (switch_vif_chanctx, or change_chanctx)
left it on the old channel: ath11k_mac_update_vif_chan() only had a TODO.
get_survey marks rx_channel [in use], and the BSS channel survey is only
read for rx_channel, so after every switch `iw survey dump` showed the old
channel in use with its counters frozen.

OpenWrt applies a channel change with a CSA (`wifi reload` after changing
the channel: AP-CSA-FINISHED), so every channel change hit this. The
RD03v2 Wi-Fi watchdog reads the [in use] entry and took the frozen counter
for a stuck radio: "survey freeze", and past 10 minutes of uptime it
reloaded ath11k (bench, v2.0.0-rc1: 2.4 GHz from channel 6 to 11, and
5 GHz from 52 back to 36). A firmware restart re-adds the context, which is
why one cleared it.

Set rx_channel to the new context's channel, as add_chanctx does. ath11k
runs one channel per radio here, so the first vif's new context is the
radio's channel.

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
@@ -8016,7 +8016,16 @@
 	 * should be available for the channel switch now.
 	 */
 
-	/* TODO: Update ar->rx_channel */
+	/* The vdevs move to the new context: rx_channel follows, as
+	 * add_chanctx sets it. get_survey marks that channel [in use] and the
+	 * BSS channel survey is only read for it; left on the old channel, the
+	 * survey of the channel in use stops moving after every switch.
+	 */
+	if (n_vifs > 0) {
+		spin_lock_bh(&ar->data_lock);
+		ar->rx_channel = vifs[0].new_ctx->def.chan;
+		spin_unlock_bh(&ar->data_lock);
+	}
 
 	for (i = 0; i < n_vifs; i++) {
 		arvif = ath11k_vif_to_arvif(vifs[i].vif);
